{"actor":{"display":"Evin Tunador","id":"evintunador@gmail.com","type":"human"},"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"310\t2. It appears to place `tool_use`/`tool_result`-shaped blocks directly inside `message.content`,\n311\t   which — per §5 — we did not observe in any real Codex output and recommend avoiding in favor\n312\t   of plain-text folding, which is verified to work.\n313\t\n314\t---\n315\t\n316\t## 8. Unknowns / risks for the implementer\n317\t\n318\t1. **Version drift**: this spec is pinned to codex-cli 0.144.6. Codex's rollout schema has\n319\t   clearly evolved rapidly (multi-agent fields, `custom_tool_call`, `world_state`, `compacted`\n320\t   are all plausibly recent additions) — treat this format as internal/unstable, not a public\n321\t   API. Re-verify against the installed `codex --version` before relying on this in production;\n322\t   the minimal schema in §3 is the safest bet for forward/backward compatibility since it's the\n323\t   smallest surface area.\n324\t2. **`function_call`/`custom_tool_call` replay safety** is unverified (see §5) — don't use them\n325\t   for fabrication without separately testing against the target Codex version.\n326\t3. **Picker/`--all` cwd-filtering exact matching logic** (prefix? exact string? normalized path?)\n327\t   was not tested beyond reading the `--help` text; only direct `resume <id>` was verified.\n328\t4. **Very large fabricated histories**: untested whether extremely large fabricated\n329\t   `response_item` sequences (e.g. thousands of turns) hit any different code path (e.g.\n330\t   automatic `compacted` triggering, size limits) versus the small 2-3 turn case we verified.\n331\t5. **`base_instructions`**: real files carry a large (~18KB) system prompt string per session.\n332\t   Omitting it entirely worked in our test (Codex presumably falls back to its own current\n333\t   default system prompt for the *new* turn, while the fabricated *history* turns are still\n334\t   replayed as-is) — but this means the fabricated session's replayed history won't perfectly\n335\t   match \"what system prompt was actually active\" if that matters for turnbridge's use case.\n336\t   Not a blocker for the stated goal (make prior context visible), just noted for completeness.\n337\t6. **`encrypted_content` for `reasoning` items**: confirmed unfabricatable and confirmed safe to\n338\t   omit outright (don't emit `reasoning` response_items at all rather than trying to synthesize\n339\t   fake ciphertext, which would almost certainly be rejected or ignored as garbage by the API if\n340\t   it were even parsed — untested, but omission is the verified-safe path).\n341\t7. **Interactive TUI resume** was only verified to \"not error and begin rendering\" via a pty\n342\t   wrapper (`script`); we could not capture/assert on the actual rendered TUI content\n343\t   programmatically. `codex exec resume --json` is the fully-verified, content-confirmed path\n344\t   and should be turnbridge's primary target/test surface; treat TUI-resume compatibility as\n345\t   \"very likely fine, same underlying loader\" rather than independently proven line-for-line.\n346\t\n347\t---\n348\t\n349\t## 9. Verification experiment log (for reproducibility)\n350\t\n351\t1. Created scratch git repo at a scratchpad path, one commit.\n352\t2. Generated a UUIDv7 id, wrote a 3-line minimal `session_meta` + user `message` + assistant\n353\t   `message` file to `~/.codex/sessions/2026/07/18/rollout-<local-ts>-<uuid>.jsonl` (planted\n354\t   fact: \"secret code word is BANANA77\").\n355\t3. Ran `codex exec resume <id> \"Reply with exactly the secret code word...\" --json\n356\t   --skip-git-repo-check` from the scratch cwd → model correctly answered `BANANA77`.\n357\t4. Stripped the schema further (removed `session_id`, `source`, `thread_source`,\n358\t   `model_provider`, `history_mode`, `context_window`; kept only `id`, `timestamp`, `cwd`,\n359\t   `originator`, `cli_version`) with a new planted fact `PLUMFISH42` → still worked, from the\n360\t   scratch cwd.\n361\t5. Re-ran the same resume from `/tmp` (different cwd than fabricated `session_meta.cwd`) →\n362\t   still worked, proving direct-id resume ignores cwd matching.\n363\t6. Asked the resumed agent to run `pwd` (with `--dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox`) →\n364\t   printed `/tmp` (the real invocation cwd), confirming `session_meta.cwd` is not authoritative\n365\t   for execution root.\n366\t7. Regenerated with a plain `uuid4()` (non-time-ordered) id → still worked.\n367\t8. Tested interactive `codex resume <id>` with stdin redirected from `/dev/null` → immediate\n368\t   `Error: stdin is not a terminal` (no tty). Re-tested wrapped in macOS `script -q` to allocate\n369\t   a pty → process started emitting terminal-capability query escape sequences (bracketed paste\n370\t   mode, cursor position query, etc.), i.e. began normal TUI startup rather than erroring on the\n371\t   fabricated file; killed the backgrounded process after a few seconds (interactive rendering\n372\t   isn't scriptably assertable, but no crash/parse-error occurred).\n373\t9. Deleted all fabricated files/dirs after each test; verified the real\n374\t   `~/.codex/sessions/2026/07/18/` directory still contains exactly its original 22 files and\n375\t   no fabricated content remains anywhere under `~/.codex/sessions/` (`grep -rl` for planted\n376\t   secrets found nothing).\n377\t","tool_use_id":"toolu_01WMbyr77H9E7rMMbS6imUbm","type":"tool_result"}],"role":"user"},"context":{"branch":"worktree-spec-verification-gaps","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge/.claude/worktrees/spec-verification-gaps","head":"4751278e2d8945d1106edaf5990d13b3e1f330a8","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/turnbridge.git"},"conversation":{"id":"claude-code:a6b0721f-3d36-4802-bc44-520ed792e1b6","seq":51},"id":"ev1-20a31ff1a315a52d8bec47cc53b70af83859f7170d42d1d012fe8804a12f2fa2","kind":"conversation_turn","occurred_at":"2026-07-27T06:52:15.885Z","producer"