{"actor":{"id":"claude-opus-5","type":"agent"},"content":{"blocks":[{"text":"Here's the full gap list for roadmap item 3. I read both spec docs' \"Unknowns\" sections (`claude-session-format.md` §7, `codex-rollout-format.md` §8), both fabricators, `resume.ts`, and compared real on-disk timestamps from both CLIs. Two things I found while scoping change the shape of this: one live defect and one missing warning.\n\n## Group A — the roadmap's four, expanded (recommend approving)\n\n**G1. Large/long histories.** Untested on both sides; all spec verification used 2–3 line files.\n- Claude: unknown line-count/byte cliff; resume reconstructs history by walking `parentUuid` backward from the last line, so a long chain is the untested path.\n- Codex: unknown whether a big fabricated `response_item` sequence trips auto-compaction or size limits.\n- **Missing warning, found while scoping:** `resume.ts:142` only reports estimated token size on the **bootstrap** path. The **fabrication** path — the default — launches with no size warning at all. `estimateTokens` already exists in `transcript.ts`; it's just not wired to fabrication. That's a fix regardless of what the probe finds.\n\n**G2. Timestamp ordering — this one is a live defect, not just an unknown.** Both adapters stamp the import-notice line (and Codex's `session_meta`) with `now.toISOString()`, then stamp every history line with the historical `event.occurred_at`. So line 1 is always *newer* than lines 2..N — every fabricated file we ship is non-monotonic by construction (`claude-code.ts:117` vs `:138`; `codex.ts:147,162` vs `:177`). The Claude spec explicitly flags ordering tolerance as unconfirmed and recommends monotonic timestamps. It evidently doesn't hard-fail, but we've never checked what it costs us (picker sort position, ordering in the reconstructed context).\n\n**G3. Timestamp format tolerance.** Narrower than the spec implies, and I can tighten the claim: both CLIs emit millisecond-precision `...Z` on real files today, and the ledger passes `occurred_at` through verbatim with only a `Date.parse` check (`schema.js:89`) — which happily accepts second-precision and `+00:00` offsets. So a source CLI that ever changes precision flows straight into a fabricated file down a path neither CLI has been tested on. The fix is a normalization at fabricate time, not just a test.\n\n**G4. `sessionId` inline vs. filename mismatch.** Currently identical by construction (`claude-code.ts:214,219`), so the risk is regression, not present behavior. Cheapest of the set: an assertion test locking the invariant, plus one live probe to learn whether mismatch actually breaks resume or is silently ignored.\n\n**G5. Duplicate / dangling `uuid` and `parentUuid`.** Also mitigated by construction (fresh `randomUUID` per line, chained at `:148,176`), also untested. Same shape as G4: an invariant test, plus a probe for what a dangling `parentUuid` does — that's the failure mode a future partial-history or lineage-splice feature would hit first.\n\n## Group B — adjacent spec unknowns, *not* in the roadmap's four (flagging; I'd defer)\n\n**G6.** Codex `function_call`/`custom_tool_call` replay safety (§8.2) — we currently sidestep it by folding tool calls to labeled text. Only worth testing if we want structured tool history in Codex targets, which is a feature, not a gap.\n**G7.** Claude resume-picker title source (§7) — inferred, never confirmed. Cosmetic.\n**G8.** Codex picker `--all` cwd-filtering logic (§8.3) — only direct `resume <id>` was verified. Doesn't affect fabrication.\n**G9.** Codex `base_instructions` omission (§8.5) — spec already calls it a non-blocker.\n\n## Questions before I start\n\n1. **Budget for live probing.** G1 is the expensive one — finding a real size cliff means fabricating progressively larger histories and launching both CLIs against them, burning real tokens on both accounts. Do you want me to genuinely hunt for the cliff, or cap it at a realistic ceiling (say ~500 turns / a few hundred KB), confirm it's clean there, and document \"verified to N, unknown beyond\"? I lean toward the cap — the cliff's exact location is less useful than knowing our real-world range is safe.\n\n2. **G2: fix or just characterize?** I'd make the notice inherit the first event's timestamp (minus 1ms) so files are monotonic, and keep `now` only for `session_meta`. That's a behavior change to shipped output, so I want your call before doing it rather than after.\n\n3. **Group B — deny outright?** You mentioned some may be out of scope. My read: G6 is a feature request in disguise, G7–G9 are cosmetic or off the fabrication path. Happy to drop all four and leave the spec docs' unknowns sections annotated as deliberately-deferred.\n\nneeds input: approve/deny G1–G5 (and confirm Group B is dropped), plus answers on live-probe budget for G1 and whether to fix the G2 timestamp ordering defect.","type":"text"}],"role":"assistant"},"context":{"branch":"worktree-spec-verification-gaps","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge/.claude/worktrees/spec-verification-gaps","head":"4751278e2d8945d1106edaf5990d13b3e1f330a8","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/turnbridge.git"},"conversation":{"id":"claude-code:a6b0721f-3d36-4802-bc44-520ed792e1b6","seq":98},"id":"ev1-49f72e8d8b3b2560a860e5879c811edf563b4107085b5c1d71c2cd0f57c4f71e","kind":"conversation_turn","occurred_at":"2026-07-27T06:54:06.249Z","producer":{"session_id":"a6b0721f-3d36-4802-bc44-520ed792e1b6","source":"claude-code","tool":"cledger","version":"0.11.0"},"raw":{"data":{"cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge","effort":"high","entrypoint":"cli","gitBranch":"main","isSidechain":false,"message":{"content":[{"text":"Here's the full gap list for roadmap item 3. I read both spec docs' \"Unknowns\" sections (`claude-session-format.md` §7, `codex-rollout-format.md` §8), both fabricators, `resume.ts`, and compared real on-disk timestamps from both CLIs. Two things I found while scoping cha